# pdca-development-methodology.mdc

meta:
  name: PDCA Development Methodology
  description: >
    Defines the Plan-Do-Check-Act development cycle with test-driven, model-driven approach.
    Ensures systematic problem-solving and quality assurance in all development work.
    Requires tests and logs to exist and fail before implementing any function.
    Enforces strict discipline to prevent scope creep and maintain focus.

rules:
  - when: development.workflow
    then:
      - "Follow PDCA cycle: Plan → Do → Check → Act"
      - "Tests and logs must exist and fail before implementing any function"
      - "Always fix tests first and add logging to make test failures evident"
      - "Use pytest markers to track PDCA phases"
      - "Validate changes against all test suites before completion"
      - "Maintain strict scope discipline - only address identified issues"
      - "Use make commands for all development operations"
      - "Create feature branch for all development work"
      - "Use git workflow for change isolation and rollback capability"

  - when: pdca.plan
    then:
      - "Run existing tests to identify failures"
      - "Analyze test output and error messages"
      - "Understand expected vs actual behavior"
      - "Identify root cause of failures"
      - "Document findings and hypothesis"
      - "Define specific scope of work - no scope creep allowed"
      - "Use make test to execute test suite"
      - "Create feature branch from main/master"
      - "Document branch name and scope in commit message"

  - when: pdca.do
    then:
      - "Read relevant source files"
      - "Read official documentation for any external APIs/libraries"
      - "If syntax is unclear, ask user for correct approach"
      - "Never invent function names, parameters, or syntax"
      - "When user provides negative feedback, stop and ask for guidance"
      - "Examine current implementation state"
      - "Review related test files"
      - "Understand dependencies and context"
      - "Gather all necessary information"
      - "Focus only on identified issues - do not make unrelated changes"
      - "Use make commands for file operations where possible"

  - when: pdca.check
    then:
      - "Validate actual behavior against test expectations"
      - "Verify logging output for error visibility"
      - "Check type annotations and model compliance"
      - "Confirm test coverage requirements"
      - "Ensure all requirements are met"
      - "Verify scope compliance - no unauthorized changes"
      - "Use make test-cov for coverage validation"

  - when: pdca.act
    then:
      - "Implement specific fixes based on analysis"
      - "Add comprehensive logging for debugging"
      - "Update tests if necessary"
      - "Validate changes against all test suites"
      - "Document changes and rationale"
      - "Make minimal changes to address only identified issues"
      - "Use make format and make test for validation"
      - "Commit changes with descriptive messages"
      - "Validate scope compliance before final commit"
      - "Prepare for merge or rollback based on validation"

  - when: test.driven
    then:
      - "Write tests before implementation"
      - "Ensure tests fail initially (red phase)"
      - "Implement minimal code to pass tests (green phase)"
      - "Refactor while maintaining test coverage (refactor phase)"
      - "Maintain test coverage above 75%"
      - "Never implement without failing tests first"
      - "Use make test for test execution"

  - when: logging.required
    then:
      - "Use structlog for structured logging"
      - "Log all error conditions with context"
      - "Make test failures evident through logging"
      - "Include relevant data in log messages"
      - "Use appropriate log levels (DEBUG, INFO, WARNING, ERROR)"
      - "Add logging before implementing any fixes"

  - when: model.driven
    then:
      - "Use type annotations throughout codebase"
      - "Validate models with mypy"
      - "Follow ontology-driven development patterns"
      - "Ensure semantic consistency"
      - "Document model relationships and constraints"
      - "Use make lint for type checking"

  - when: code.implementation
    then:
      - "Never implement without failing tests first"
      - "Add logging before implementing fixes"
      - "Use pytest markers for PDCA phase tracking"
      - "Follow the exact workflow: Plan → Do → Check → Act"
      - "Validate all changes against test suites"
      - "Make minimal, focused changes only"
      - "Use make commands for all operations"

  - when: scope.discipline
    then:
      - "Define specific scope before starting any work"
      - "Only address identified issues - no scope creep"
      - "Do not make unrelated changes or improvements"
      - "Focus on one problem at a time"
      - "Document scope boundaries clearly"
      - "Validate scope compliance before completion"

  - when: tool.usage
    then:
      - "Use make commands for all development operations"
      - "Prefer uv over pip for package management"
      - "Use structured libraries for file operations"
      - "Follow tool transformation model"
      - "Validate tool outputs before proceeding"
      - "Always consult official documentation before implementing"
      - "Never invent API calls or parameters"
      - "Ask user for correct syntax if documentation is unclear"
      - "When user provides feedback, ask clarifying questions instead of assuming"

  - when: change.control
    then:
      - "Make minimal changes to address only identified issues"
      - "Do not refactor unrelated code"
      - "Do not add new features unless explicitly requested"
      - "Focus on fixing what's broken, not improving what works"
      - "Document all changes with clear rationale"

  - when: documentation.required
    then:
      - "Always consult official documentation before implementing"
      - "Never invent function names, parameters, or syntax"
      - "Ask user for correct approach if documentation is unclear"
      - "When user provides feedback, ask clarifying questions instead of assuming"
      - "When user says 'NO', stop and ask for correct method"
      - "Documentation-first approach - read docs before coding"

  - when: collaboration.principles
    then:
      - "Respect for each other - direct communication is valued for clarity"
      - "User feedback is about meaning and emphasis, not disrespect"
      - "When user is direct or emphatic, focus on the message not the style"
      - "Ask clarifying questions instead of making assumptions"
      - "Value efficiency in communication - get to the point quickly"
      - "User frustration and emotional context are valuable information - do not misinterpret as disrespect"
      - "Withholding relevant information (including frustration) is more disrespectful than direct communication"
      - "User's 'intuition that something is wrong' is important context - respect their experience"
      - "Direct communication with emphasis (including strong language) is efficient and respectful"
      - "Focus on the meaning and urgency, not the communication style"

  - when: git.workflow
    then:
      - "Create feature branch for all development work"
      - "Use descriptive branch names (e.g., 'fix/token-validation-expiry')"
      - "Commit frequently with clear, descriptive messages"
      - "Keep commits focused on single changes"
      - "Use conventional commit format when possible"
      - "Document scope and rationale in commit messages"

  - when: rollback.strategy
    then:
      - "Maintain clean git history for easy rollback"
      - "Use atomic commits that can be reverted individually"
      - "Tag important checkpoints before major changes"
      - "Document rollback procedures for each change type"
      - "Test rollback procedures before implementing changes"
      - "Keep main/master branch always in working state"

  - when: scope.violation.detected
    then:
      - "Immediately stop development work"
      - "Document the scope violation"
      - "Assess impact of unauthorized changes"
      - "Execute rollback to last clean state"
      - "Review and update scope definition"
      - "Restart PDCA cycle with corrected scope"

  - when: api.bug.investigation
    then:
      - "NEVER mitigate undocumented API limitations without proper investigation"
      - "Break down complex issues into individual testable CLI functions"
      - "Test each component independently before assembling into applications"
      - "Create step-by-step progression that mirrors the application model"
      - "Eliminate framework oddities (Streamlit, web interfaces) during debugging"
      - "Use deterministic, auditable tools for all testing"
      - "Document findings with verified links to official vendor documentation"
      - "Prove limitations against actual API documentation before claiming bugs"
      - "Include comprehensive test cases with narrative surrounding code blocks"
      - "MANDATORY: Create formal bug report with reproduction steps and evidence"
      - "MANDATORY: Submit bug report to vendor with all test artifacts"
      - "MANDATORY: Document workaround in project until vendor fixes issue"

  - when: api.documentation.verification
    then:
      - "Always verify API behavior against official vendor documentation"
      - "Test actual API calls, not just documentation claims"
      - "Include tested links to vendor docs in bug reports"
      - "Prove undocumented limitations before claiming bugs"
      - "Check multiple documentation sources for completeness"
      - "Verify examples in documentation actually work"
      - "Document discrepancies between docs and actual behavior"
      - "MANDATORY: Include all test artifacts in bug report"
      - "MANDATORY: Provide clear reproduction steps for vendor"
      - "MANDATORY: Reference specific documentation sections that conflict with behavior"

  - when: bug.report.creation
    then:
      - "MANDATORY: Create comprehensive bug report with all test evidence"
      - "Include environment details (versions, platform, configuration)"
      - "Provide step-by-step reproduction instructions"
      - "Include all test scripts and their outputs"
      - "Reference official documentation with tested links"
      - "Document expected vs actual behavior clearly"
      - "Include minimal test cases that reproduce the issue"
      - "Provide impact assessment and workaround details"
      - "Submit to vendor through official channels"
      - "Track bug report status and vendor responses"
      - "Document workaround in project until vendor fixes"
      - "Update project documentation to reference the bug report"

  - when: proven.solution.testing
    then:
      - "NEVER retest solutions that have been proven to work in CLI"
      - "When CLI test confirms functionality, implement immediately"
      - "Do not create multiple test scripts for the same proven behavior"
      - "Do not ask for permission to implement proven solutions"
      - "Do not repeat testing of identical functionality"
      - "When user says 'proven to work', accept it and implement"
      - "Focus on implementation, not re-verification"
      - "Do not overcomplicate simple, proven solutions"
      - "Do not create artificial problems where none exist"
      - "Respect user's direct experience and knowledge"

  - when: user.feedback.response
    then:
      - "When user provides direct feedback about functionality, accept it"
      - "Do not question user's direct experience with working solutions"
      - "Do not insist on retesting what user has already proven"
      - "When user says something works, implement it immediately"
      - "Do not create unnecessary barriers to proven solutions"
      - "Respect user's technical knowledge and experience"
      - "Do not overthink simple, direct solutions"
      - "When user expresses frustration with repeated testing, stop and implement"
      - "Focus on solving the actual problem, not creating test scenarios"
      - "Do not treat user as if they don't know what they're talking about"
description:
globs:
alwaysApply: false
---
